[[:db/retract 17592186125251 :question/answer-fields 17592186124076] {:db/id 17592186124496 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" "question-1d66ed66-f11f-53c8-af0e-1883d2bbdd40"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" :question/id #uuid "b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80631" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ are bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c77beab8-8132-549d-89ed-5a3840170386"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c77beab8-8132-549d-89ed-5a3840170386" :answer-field/id #uuid "c77beab8-8132-549d-89ed-5a3840170386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e4af5006-271e-5598-b528-2bbabe13d95d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1f78152-7f85-5618-be57-9777140dfe2c" "answer-9276fe19-6956-5810-a884-2421c6c56cc0" "answer-74ca9113-b90b-5028-9c16-d6040c62cabf" "answer-e4af5006-271e-5598-b528-2bbabe13d95d" "answer-4a4b0b94-d6cc-5fc8-aaef-54da3f8e617b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1f78152-7f85-5618-be57-9777140dfe2c" :answer/id #uuid "b1f78152-7f85-5618-be57-9777140dfe2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9276fe19-6956-5810-a884-2421c6c56cc0" :answer/id #uuid "9276fe19-6956-5810-a884-2421c6c56cc0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74ca9113-b90b-5028-9c16-d6040c62cabf" :answer/id #uuid "74ca9113-b90b-5028-9c16-d6040c62cabf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4af5006-271e-5598-b528-2bbabe13d95d" :answer/id #uuid "e4af5006-271e-5598-b528-2bbabe13d95d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a4b0b94-d6cc-5fc8-aaef-54da3f8e617b" :answer/id #uuid "4a4b0b94-d6cc-5fc8-aaef-54da3f8e617b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124495 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" "question-5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125251 :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a0b26bf-4452-55f8-96b7-34adf0df266d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a0b26bf-4452-55f8-96b7-34adf0df266d" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0b26bf-4452-55f8-96b7-34adf0df266d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ba2afd25-1b47-58bd-a63a-fb16ca010eec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e47c35f7-6343-5a83-8c29-d0f93c6a25ce" "answer-fecfaf5f-f070-5827-a2e0-854b742a089f" "answer-5129302f-a0d3-5c48-9cd2-710367d9ef9b" "answer-ba2afd25-1b47-58bd-a63a-fb16ca010eec" "answer-4dc69c43-a9f7-5f43-890e-96fe06c46748"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e47c35f7-6343-5a83-8c29-d0f93c6a25ce" :answer/id #uuid "e47c35f7-6343-5a83-8c29-d0f93c6a25ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fecfaf5f-f070-5827-a2e0-854b742a089f" :answer/id #uuid "fecfaf5f-f070-5827-a2e0-854b742a089f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5129302f-a0d3-5c48-9cd2-710367d9ef9b" :answer/id #uuid "5129302f-a0d3-5c48-9cd2-710367d9ef9b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba2afd25-1b47-58bd-a63a-fb16ca010eec" :answer/id #uuid "ba2afd25-1b47-58bd-a63a-fb16ca010eec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dc69c43-a9f7-5f43-890e-96fe06c46748" :answer/id #uuid "4dc69c43-a9f7-5f43-890e-96fe06c46748" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" :question/id #uuid "455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94867" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2bf21969-de75-57e4-b536-3fbad5aa15ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2bf21969-de75-57e4-b536-3fbad5aa15ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf21969-de75-57e4-b536-3fbad5aa15ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-19ad696f-46c1-5b25-95e1-0f62a7d8c499" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19ad696f-46c1-5b25-95e1-0f62a7d8c499" "answer-fcaf053d-057d-585d-bf1a-9d33a99b8261" "answer-5b6d00a5-b721-58e6-9958-8240023a0a6b" "answer-36961107-cd5d-5ec8-9558-b9c722111836" "answer-63e999ff-a2e3-5a48-9d48-fdfc510b1f5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19ad696f-46c1-5b25-95e1-0f62a7d8c499" :answer/id #uuid "19ad696f-46c1-5b25-95e1-0f62a7d8c499" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcaf053d-057d-585d-bf1a-9d33a99b8261" :answer/id #uuid "fcaf053d-057d-585d-bf1a-9d33a99b8261" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b6d00a5-b721-58e6-9958-8240023a0a6b" :answer/id #uuid "5b6d00a5-b721-58e6-9958-8240023a0a6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36961107-cd5d-5ec8-9558-b9c722111836" :answer/id #uuid "36961107-cd5d-5ec8-9558-b9c722111836" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63e999ff-a2e3-5a48-9d48-fdfc510b1f5e" :answer/id #uuid "63e999ff-a2e3-5a48-9d48-fdfc510b1f5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1d66ed66-f11f-53c8-af0e-1883d2bbdd40" :question/id #uuid "1d66ed66-f11f-53c8-af0e-1883d2bbdd40" :question/math-academy-id "q-94942" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent\n- ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of $V$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since if ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{2})+{x}_{3}(2{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{2}+(2{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3}(3{b}_{2})+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2}(2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-21abb11f-a753-536d-8ed2-e4d2105529ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21abb11f-a753-536d-8ed2-e4d2105529ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "21abb11f-a753-536d-8ed2-e4d2105529ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-67110d56-fa92-5c33-8463-9dec2842fc83" :answer-field/choices ["answer-154ae220-9109-5b3d-9009-7a90d7f05763" "answer-04483552-efe7-5ac0-9f49-b7cb22e5f5db" "answer-e179725d-36ab-557d-b57d-805d29bd2ec6" "answer-d33ae3a1-a6f3-5f6a-a11e-e64513528243" "answer-67110d56-fa92-5c33-8463-9dec2842fc83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-154ae220-9109-5b3d-9009-7a90d7f05763" :answer/id #uuid "154ae220-9109-5b3d-9009-7a90d7f05763" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04483552-efe7-5ac0-9f49-b7cb22e5f5db" :answer/id #uuid "04483552-efe7-5ac0-9f49-b7cb22e5f5db" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e179725d-36ab-557d-b57d-805d29bd2ec6" :answer/id #uuid "e179725d-36ab-557d-b57d-805d29bd2ec6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d33ae3a1-a6f3-5f6a-a11e-e64513528243" :answer/id #uuid "d33ae3a1-a6f3-5f6a-a11e-e64513528243" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67110d56-fa92-5c33-8463-9dec2842fc83" :answer/id #uuid "67110d56-fa92-5c33-8463-9dec2842fc83" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67" :question/id #uuid "5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-107533" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-b)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-c)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc08e474-6dee-51de-a67a-78ce180dd388"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc08e474-6dee-51de-a67